    {
      "conclusion_id": "request-cache-pool-ownership",
      "title": "Distinguish request bookkeeping, cache retention and reusable pool capacity",
      "claims": [
        "At the pinned vLLM revision, pop_blocks_for_free removes request bookkeeping without returning blocks to the pool. BlockPool.free_blocks decrements references and requeues zero-reference non-null blocks. Cached hashes are evicted when get_new_blocks selects a block with caching enabled; these methods do not demonstrate physical device-memory deallocation.",
        "At the pinned SGLang revision, checkpoint rewrites request-to-token mappings, moves the request lock, and runs component cleanup. Session reference registration requires enable_session_radix_cache, is_finished, and a finish reason that is not FINISH_ABORT. _drain_device_frees dispatches FreeComponentDeviceSlot actions, whose downstream implementation is outside this accepted subset.",
        "At the pinned TGI revision, FilterBatch pops, filters and reinserts cached batch state. SimpleAllocator.free extends a free-block list; RadixAllocator.free decreases a prefix reference, may insert uncached prefill tokens into the trie, returns duplicate/non-prefill blocks to its free list, and leaves retained prefix blocks to later trie eviction. These are ownership and allocator observations, not measured HBM release."
      ],
      "diagnostic_use": "Inspect request references, cache-held references and pool free capacity as separate counters; do not infer a leak or successful physical deallocation solely from request completion or cache membership.",
